  "vulnerabilities": [
    {
      "cve": "CVE-2026-110910",
      "ids": [
        {
          "system_name": "SUSE CVE Page",
          "text": "https://www.suse.com/security/cve/CVE-2026-110910"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "category": "general",
          "text": "### Summary\n\n\n\n`qs.stringify` with `allowEmptyArrays: true` writes the key of an empty array without encoding it, even though keys are encoded by default. A key that contains `\u0026` (or the configured `delimiter`), `=`, or `#` therefore becomes query string syntax instead of data, so a caller-supplied key name can add parameters to the serialized query string, or cut it short.\n\n\n\n### Details\n\n\n\nIn `lib/stringify.js`, the `allowEmptyArrays` branch returns `adjustedPrefix + '[]'` early, before the code that passes every other key through `encoder` and `formatter`. So `{ \"x\u0026k=v\": [\"1\"] }` serializes as `x%26k%3Dv%5B0%5D=1`, but `{ \"x\u0026k=v\": [] }` serializes as `x\u0026k=v[]`. The early return applies at any depth (`{ \"a\": { \"b\u0026k=v\": [] } }` gives `a[b\u0026k=v][]`), under every `arrayFormat`, with `allowDots`, and with a custom `encoder`, which is never called for that key. With `encode: false` or `encodeValuesOnly: true`, keys are intentionally left unencoded on every path; those configurations are not affected.\n\n\n\n#### PoC\n\n\n\n```js\n\n\n\nvar qs = require(\"qs\");\n\n\n\nvar query = qs.stringify({ q: \"hi\", \"x\u0026apiKey=evil\u0026y\": [] }, { allowEmptyArrays: true });\n\n\n\n// \"q=hi\u0026x\u0026apiKey=evil\u0026y[]\"\n\n\n\nqs.parse(query);\n\n\n\n// { q: \"hi\", x: \"\", apiKey: \"evil\", y: [\"\"] }\n\n\n\nqs.stringify({ \"x#\": [], z: \"1\" }, { allowEmptyArrays: true });\n\n\n\n// \"x#[]\u0026z=1\": in a URL, everything after \"#\" becomes the fragment\n\n\n\n```\n\n\n\n#### Fix\n\n\n\nApplied in 234d505 on `main` and released as v6.17.0, and backported to v6.16.2, v6.15.4, v6.14.3, v6.13.4, and v6.12.6: the key of an empty array goes through `encoder` and `formatter` exactly as the key of any other value does, so `{ \"x\u0026apiKey=evil\u0026y\": [] }` now serializes as `x%26apiKey%3Devil%26y[]`. Keys that need no encoding serialize as before, with two exceptions that match every other path: the brackets of a nested key are now encoded (`a[b][]` becomes `a%5Bb%5D[]`, which `qs.parse` reads identically), and with `encodeDotInKeys`, a dot in the key becomes `%252E` instead of `%2E` (which `decodeDotInKeys` previously misread as nesting).\n\n\n\n### Affected versions\n\n\n\n- `\u003e=6.12.0 \u003c6.12.6`, fixed in v6.12.6\n\n\n\n- `\u003e=6.13.0 \u003c6.13.4`, fixed in v6.13.4\n\n\n\n- `\u003e=6.14.0 \u003c6.14.3`, fixed in v6.14.3\n\n\n\n- `\u003e=6.15.0 \u003c6.15.4`, fixed in v6.15.4\n\n\n\n- `\u003e=6.16.0 \u003c6.16.2`, fixed in v6.16.2\n\n\n\nv6.17.0 and later are not affected.\n\n\n\n`allowEmptyArrays` was introduced in f22b2bc and first shipped in v6.12.0, with this early return. Earlier versions have no `allowEmptyArrays` option and are not affected.\n\n\n\n### Impact\n\n\n\nAn application that passes an object whose key names come from untrusted input to `qs.stringify` with `allowEmptyArrays: true` (for example, to forward a parsed JSON body or user-selected fields to an upstream service) can have parameters injected into the resulting query string. The injected parameters are not keys of the object, so deleting or overwriting a key on the object (such as setting a trusted `apiKey` after copying the user's fields) does not remove them; whether the attacker's value or the application's wins depends on how the receiving side handles a duplicate parameter. A `#` in such a key ends the query string early when the result is placed in a URL. `allowEmptyArrays` is off by default, and only keys whose value (after any `filter`) is an empty array are affected.",
          "title": "CVE description"
        }
      ],
